TITLE:: PltEnvelope
summary:: An Env drawn with its breakpoints, curves and release node
categories:: Libraries>PlotLib
related:: Overviews/PlotLib, Classes/Env, Classes/PltWave

DESCRIPTION::
link::Classes/Env#-plot:: already exists; this one is here for the same reason as
the rest of the library: axes in seconds and level, the breakpoints marked, and the
release node shown, so a sustaining envelope reads differently from a fixed one.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
argument:: env
argument:: title
argument:: width
argument:: height

METHOD:: points
The curve as plain data, code::[times, values]::, sampled so that any curve type is
drawn as the shape it actually is.

note::
The breakpoint times are added to the even samples. A corner that falls between two
samples is cut off: sampling code::Env.adsr:: 50 times evenly returned a peak of
0.965 for an envelope that reaches 1.
::

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: env
METHOD:: breakpointTimes
Cumulative time of each breakpoint, which is where the dots go.
METHOD:: showPoints
METHOD:: resolution
METHOD:: curveColorKey
METHOD:: pointColorKey
METHOD:: times
METHOD:: values

EXAMPLES::
code::
PltEnvelope(Env.perc(0.01, 0.4), "Env.perc").front;
PltEnvelope(Env.adsr(0.02, 0.2, 0.4, 0.5), "Env.adsr").front;
(
PltEnvelope(Env([0, 1, 0.3, 0.8, 0], [0.1, 0.3, 0.2, 0.6],
	[\lin, \exp, \sine, -4]), "mixed curves").front;
)

// what a curve value actually does
PltEnvelope(Env([0, 1, 0], [0.5, 0.5], -8), "curve -8").front;
PltEnvelope(Env([0, 1, 0], [0.5, 0.5], 8), "curve 8").front;
::
